    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d167">

                <pb facs="0199" n="185" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg185"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 [Gauss, Clarence E.]: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-01T19:00:00-04:00">November 1, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1412. From Grew. On October 30 Service informed me that you were going to resign
                    as of November 1, that your decision was final and that you had asked him to
                    inform officers of the Department.<anchor n="2a" xml:id="d167-f1"/> That evening
                    I so informed the Acting Secretary who informed the President.</p>
                <p>On October 31 the report was published in the morning editions of New York and
                    Washington papers that you had submitted your resignation <pb facs="0200"
                        n="186" xml:id="pg186"/> nation as a result of General Stilwell’s removal.
                    Our 1399, October 30, 8 p.m.,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d167-f2"/> was sent you
                    before this publicity broke.</p>
                <p>The afternoon of October 31 the President at his press conference in reply to
                    specific questions by a press correspondent said that the State Department had
                    told him that you were resigning; the President added that you had wanted to do
                    so for a long time; that you would most certainly be appointed to a new post;
                    and that there was no connection between your resignation and Stilwell’s
                    recall.</p>
                <p>The decision you have taken is a matter of sincere regret to us. [Grew.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d167-f1" n="2a" xml:id="d167-f1-content">
                <p>John S. Service, Second Secretary of Embassy in China, was temporarily in
                    Washington for consultation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d167-f2" n="3" xml:id="d167-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; it requested the Ambassador to postpone his resignation.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
